{"episodes":{"200881":"Thunderstorms developed through the afternoon and continued into the evening as a cold front moved through the Ohio Valley.","200882":"Thunderstorms developed through the afternoon and continued into the evening as a cold front moved through the Ohio Valley.","200883":"Thunderstorms developed through the afternoon and continued into the evening as a cold front moved through the Ohio Valley.","201416":"Storms developed along and in advance of a cold front. Some of these storms became strong enough to produce damaging winds and hail.","201623":"Damaging wind gusts and an instance of large hail swept through eastern Kentucky on the afternoon of April 14th as a low pressure system passed through the Great Lakes region. Main impacts from the brief stint of severe weather included downed trees around Greenup County and quarter sized hail outside of the Letitia township. Storms quickly ventured into West Virginia for the rest of the afternoon and evening.","202089":"On April 14th, strong thunderstorms traversed through southeast Ohio in response to an advancing low pressure system. Storms grew tall enough to produce golf ball and baseball  size hail in Lawrence County, which resulted in damage to vehicles in the area. Damaging wind gusts within these storms also produced tree and structural damage in Gallia County on the afternoon of the 14th. Activity quickly advanced east of the Ohio River into West Virginia for the rest of the day.","202128":"A digging low pressure system and its attendant frontal boundary served up strong thunderstorms across the state of West Virginia on the afternoon of April 14th. Storms first formed over the lower Ohio Valley during the late morning hours and then tracked into West Virginia for the afternoon and evening hours. Damaging winds and large hail were noted within the strongest storms. A swath of large hail between 1.5 to 2.25 inches fell over Cabell County and across the Kanawha Valley into the foothills of the Appalachian mountains. Tree and power line damage was also observed within the strongest storms that afternoon before activity quickly dashed east of the mountains by that evening.","202190":"Storms developed along and in advance of a cold front. Some of these storms became strong enough to produce damaging winds and hail.","206835":"A line of storms developed across Kentucky ahead of a cold front that began pushing into the state from the west during the afternoon hours on the 14th. As it continued to traverse the state and move into eastern Kentucky, the line of storms resulted in numerous instances of severe weather, including hail and damaging winds. The system them exited overnight, with storms and instances of severe weather ending just after midnight on the 15th in Harlan County."},"events":[{"begin":"2025-04-14T19:28Z","county":"Switzerland","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"1.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2025-04-14T19:30Z","endLat":38.8689,"endLon":-84.9703,"episode":200881,"id":1245781,"injuries":0,"lat":38.8689,"location":"0.53 E East Enterprise","lon":-84.9703,"magnitude":45.0,"magnitudeType":"EG","narrative":"A tree fell near the intersection of State Route 250 and Seymour Road.","source":"State Official","state":"IN","type":"Thunderstorm Wind","wfo":"ILN"},{"begin":"2025-04-14T19:34Z","county":"Boone","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"1.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2025-04-14T19:36Z","endLat":39.0,"endLon":-84.71,"episode":200882,"id":1245793,"injuries":0,"lat":39.0,"location":"2.12 SW Limaburg","lon":-84.71,"magnitude":45.0,"magnitudeType":"EG","narrative":"A tree was downed.","source":"State Official","state":"KY","type":"Thunderstorm Wind","wfo":"ILN"},{"begin":"2025-04-14T19:48Z","county":"Ohio","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2025-04-14T19:50Z","endLat":38.95,"endLon":-84.86,"episode":200881,"id":1245782,"injuries":0,"lat":38.95,"location":"0.54 E Rising Sun","lon":-84.86,"magnitude":0.75,"source":"Law Enforcement","s